<blockquote>
<p>Then, in only a couple of paragraphs, the anomaly both discovers it's damaging files and then fixes it, with no actual struggle or conflict apparent at all beyond a couple of sad emoticons. This feels pretty rushed and undeveloped and just leaves me disappointed at how the story played out, since the actual events are over and done with so much faster than the scene-setting interviews.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I was concerned about that, but after a few reviews I concluded that it would be fine, which I suppose is my bad. I want to expand upon the emotions a bit, I wasn’t entirely sure how to carry it out in a way that wouldn’t be dragging things out. In a very rough draft of this, I had another interview after an incident log, but I was afraid that it was too similar to other AI SCPs, so I decided to go a bit of a different route. Thank you for comment, I completely understand where you’re coming from.</p>

